From: SteamOS ARM alioth port
Subject: [PATCH] input: ft8756: power-cycle the controller at probe

This is the fix for the 0xEF reads, and it was found by asking the
controller the same question from the vendor driver on the stock
Android, where touch works. With root, through that driver's own
sysfs:

  Read A3: 56        <- exactly what this driver expects
  Read 9F: 52
  Read A6: 1D        <- application firmware v0x1d, running
  IC ID:0x5652       <- fts_driver_info
  of_node -> .../spi@990000/focaltech@0

So it is the same part this driver was written for, and its firmware
is in the chip. Everything earlier in this series that read 0xEF as
"a part this driver does not know" or "no application firmware" was
wrong about the chip, though the defects those patches fixed were
real.

What 0xEF means came out of reproducing it there. Writing 0x55 0xAA
(the ROM bootloader entry the id check sends on a mismatch) and then
resetting left the vendor driver reading 0xEF as well -- and then
`cat fts_hw_reset`, which resets the line and waits for the chip,
answered 0xEF however many times it ran. The chip came back to
0x56/0x52 only when the vendor's suspend/resume path ran, which turns
vdd and vddio off and then on again.

The controller leaves ROM only on a power cycle. A reset does not
take it out, and this driver only ever resets:
regulator_bulk_enable() cannot power-cycle anything, because the
rails are already up when we probe, so it takes a reference and the
chip never sees a power-on.

Both rails have exactly one consumer in the device tree -- this node,
via vio-supply = <&vreg_l10c_3p0> and ibb-supply = <&vreg_l1c_1p8>,
which are the vendor's vdd (pm8150a-l10, 3.0V) and vcc_i2c
(pm8150a-l1, 1.8V) -- so dropping our reference really does drop
them. The reset line is held low across the cycle, where it belongs:
it was taken with GPIOD_OUT_LOW earlier in probe and nothing has
released it yet.

Also logs whether each rail was already on before probe. That one
line is the measurement behind the paragraph above, and it is the
first thing to read if this does not work.

--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/ft8756.c
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/ft8756.c
@@ -614,10 +614,46 @@
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret,
 					 "Cannot get supplies: %d\n", ret);
 
+	/*
+	 * Power-cycle, do not just enable.
+	 *
+	 * This controller leaves its ROM bootloader only on a power
+	 * cycle; a reset does not take it out. Measured on this phone
+	 * through the vendor driver's own sysfs: once the chip answered
+	 * 0xEF from every register, `cat fts_hw_reset` (which resets the
+	 * line and waits) kept answering 0xEF however many times it ran,
+	 * and the chip came back to its real id -- 0xA3 = 0x56,
+	 * 0x9F = 0x52, firmware 0x1d -- only when the vendor's
+	 * suspend/resume path ran, which turns vdd and vddio off and
+	 * then on again.
+	 *
+	 * regulator_bulk_enable() alone cannot do that here: the rails
+	 * are already up when we probe, so it only takes a reference and
+	 * the chip never sees a power-on. Both rails have exactly one
+	 * consumer in the device tree (this node), so dropping our
+	 * reference really does drop them.
+	 *
+	 * The reset line is still held low at this point -- it was taken
+	 * with GPIOD_OUT_LOW above and nothing has released it yet --
+	 * which is where it belongs across a power cycle.
+	 */
+	for (int i = 0; i < FT8756_NUM_SUPPLIES; i++)
+		dev_info(dev, "supply %s was %s before probe\n",
+			 ts->supplies[i].supply,
+			 regulator_is_enabled(ts->supplies[i].consumer) ?
+				 "on" : "off");
+
 	ret = regulator_bulk_enable(FT8756_NUM_SUPPLIES, ts->supplies);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
+	regulator_bulk_disable(FT8756_NUM_SUPPLIES, ts->supplies);
+	/* Long enough for the rails to actually fall. */
+	msleep(30);
+
+	ret = regulator_bulk_enable(FT8756_NUM_SUPPLIES, ts->supplies);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
 
 	/* Delay for regulators to stabilize */
 	usleep_range(10000, 11000);
